solves: "<p>When a candidate applies for a job, the ATS records where they came from. In most systems, that means whichever link they clicked immediately before submitting — usually the career site. But candidates rarely follow a straight path.</p>
<p>A friend sends a referral link. The candidate reads the posting, closes the tab, and applies directly a week later. The ATS records career site. The referral disappears. The friend never gets credited, and someone has to figure out why the bonus didn't happen.</p>
<p>This is the attribution problem. The channel that worked is invisible. Budget decisions compound the error every quarter. Referral attribution breaks the same way, and when it does, someone has to investigate manually — creating friction with employees and credibility damage for the referral program.</p>
<p>Attribution A[i]gent fixes this by capturing every touchpoint in the candidate journey. When a candidate clicks any tracked link, a cookie records the source. That record persists across sessions. When the candidate eventually applies, the correct source writes back to the ATS automatically. The full journey — every channel, every interaction, with timestamps — is available for reporting.</p>"

